3934. Smallest Unique Subarray (Hard)

You are given an integer array nums.

Find the minimum length of a subarray that is not identical to any other subarray in nums.

Return an integer denoting the minimum possible length of such a subarray.

Two subarrays are considered identical if they have the same length and the same elements in corresponding positions.

 

Example 1:

Input: nums = [3,3,3]

Output: 3

Explanation:

    Subarrays of length 1: [3] → appears 3 times
    Subarrays of length 2: [3, 3] → appears 2 times
    Subarrays of length 3: [3, 3, 3] → appears once

The subarray [3, 3, 3] is unique, so the smallest unique subarray length is 3.

Example 2:

Input: nums = [2,1,2,3,3]

Output: 1

Explanation:

Subarrays of length 1:

    [2] → appears 2 times
    [1] → appears once
    [3] → appears 2 times

The subarray [1] is unique, so the smallest unique subarray length is 1.

Example 3:

Input: nums = [1,1,2,2,1]

Output: 2

Explanation:

Subarrays of length 1:

    [1] → appears 3 times
    [2] → appears 2 times

Subarrays of length 2:

    [1, 1] → appears once
    [1, 2] → appears once
    [2, 2] → appears once
    [2, 1] → appears once

There is at least one subarray of length 2 that is unique, so the smallest unique subarray length is 2.

 

Constraints:

    1 <= nums.length <= 10^5
    1 <= nums[i] <= 10^5



Answer:
import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public int smallestUniqueSubarray(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;

        if (n == 1) {
            return 1;
        }

        int[] suffixArray = buildSuffixArray(nums);
        int[] rank = new int[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            rank[suffixArray[i]] = i;
        }

        // lcp[r] = LCP of suffixArray[r] and suffixArray[r - 1]
        int[] lcp = new int[n];
        int h = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int r = rank[i];

            if (r == 0) {
                continue;
            }

            int j = suffixArray[r - 1];

            while (i + h < n && j + h < n
                    && nums[i + h] == nums[j + h]) {
                h++;
            }

            lcp[r] = h;

            if (h > 0) {
                h--;
            }
        }

        int answer = n;

        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {
            int start = suffixArray[r];
            int suffixLength = n - start;

            int maxLcp = lcp[r];

            // LCP with the next suffix in sorted order
            if (r + 1 < n) {
                maxLcp = Math.max(maxLcp, lcp[r + 1]);
            }

            // If the whole suffix is repeated, it has no unique
            // subarray starting at this position.
            if (maxLcp < suffixLength) {
                answer = Math.min(answer, maxLcp + 1);
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }

    private int[] buildSuffixArray(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int N = n + 1; // Include a sentinel at the end

        // Coordinate compression: values become 1..m.
        // Sentinel remains 0, smaller than every value.
        int[] sorted = nums.clone();
        Arrays.sort(sorted);

        int m = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (i == 0 || sorted[i] != sorted[i - 1]) {
                sorted[m++] = sorted[i];
            }
        }

        int[] a = new int[N];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            a[i] = Arrays.binarySearch(sorted, 0, m, nums[i]) + 1;
        }

        a[n] = 0;

        int[] p = new int[N];
        int[] c = new int[N];

        // Initial counting sort by symbol
        int[] count = new int[Math.max(N, m + 1)];

        for (int value : a) {
            count[value]++;
        }

        for (int i = 1; i < count.length; i++) {
            count[i] += count[i - 1];
        }

        for (int i = N - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            p[--count[a[i]]] = i;
        }

        int classes = 1;
        c[p[0]] = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < N; i++) {
            if (a[p[i]] != a[p[i - 1]]) {
                classes++;
            }
            c[p[i]] = classes - 1;
        }

        int[] pn = new int[N];
        int[] cn = new int[N];

        // Sort cyclic shifts by doubling
        for (int len = 1; len < N; len <<= 1) {
            for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
                pn[i] = p[i] - len;
                if (pn[i] < 0) {
                    pn[i] += N;
                }
            }

            Arrays.fill(count, 0, classes, 0);

            for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
                count[c[pn[i]]]++;
            }

            for (int i = 1; i < classes; i++) {
                count[i] += count[i - 1];
            }

            for (int i = N - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
                p[--count[c[pn[i]]]] = pn[i];
            }

            int newClasses = 1;
            cn[p[0]] = 0;

            for (int i = 1; i < N; i++) {
                int cur = p[i];
                int prev = p[i - 1];

                int midCur = (cur + len) % N;
                int midPrev = (prev + len) % N;

                if (c[cur] != c[prev]
                        || c[midCur] != c[midPrev]) {
                    newClasses++;
                }

                cn[cur] = newClasses - 1;
            }

            int[] temp = c;
            c = cn;
            cn = temp;

            classes = newClasses;
        }

        // Remove the sentinel suffix at position n
        int[] suffixArray = new int[n];
        int index = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            if (p[i] != n) {
                suffixArray[index++] = p[i];
            }
        }

        return suffixArray;
    }
}





Approach: Sliding Window + Hashing

We need to find the smallest length len such that at least one subarray of that length appears exactly once in the entire array.

Since n <= 100,000, checking every subarray directly would be too slow.

We can use a rolling hash to represent each subarray efficiently and count how many times each hash occurs.


Dry Run
Input: nums = [1, 1, 2, 2, 1]

Length 1:
[1] -> appears 3 times
[2] -> appears 2 times


No unique subarray of length 1.

Length 2:
[1,1] -> 1 time
[1,2] -> 1 time
[2,2] -> 1 time
[2,1] -> 1 time


Every subarray of length 2 is unique.

Output: 2
Complexity Analysis
- Time Complexity: \(O(n^2)\) in the worst case due to checking each length and its windows.
- Space Complexity: \(O(n)\) for the frequency map.
This approach is not optimal for the maximum constraint. A more efficient solution would use suffix arrays or suffix automata to find the shortest unique subarray in \(O(n\log n)\) or better.






